00:59:13,215 --> 00:59:14,750
In the European session?

942
00:59:14,750 --> 00:59:16,935
This is not an exhaustible list.

943
00:59:16,935 --> 00:59:22,375
What you want to do is characterize the strategy in every single possible way.

944
00:59:22,375 --> 00:59:28,585
Then, you want to define the rules and preconditions for taking on the strategy.

945
00:59:28,585 --> 00:59:31,825
So, firstly, what time of day do you execute this?

946
00:59:31,825 --> 00:59:35,075
What type of market are you executing this in?

947
00:59:35,075 --> 00:59:38,050
And what is your expectation analysis?

948
00:59:38,050 --> 00:59:41,755
Now, an expectation analysis is particularly important.

949
00:59:41,755 --> 00:59:43,170
So, what is it?

950
00:59:43,170 --> 00:59:47,140
In simple terms, it's what you would expect to see.

951
00:59:47,140 --> 00:59:50,900
So, let's take a basic example of a breakout trade.

952
00:59:50,900 --> 00:59:52,966
Now, in a breakout trade, we expect

953
00:59:52,966 --> 00:59:54,441
to see a pickup in

954
00:59:54,441 --> 00:59:59,754
volume, a pickup in the volatility, and a little bit of initiation from either the buyers or sellers.

955
00:59:59,755 --> 01:00:03,415
So, a little bit more one-sided trade as opposed to two-way trade.

956
01:00:03,415 --> 01:00:05,730
These are our expectations.

957
01:00:05,730 --> 01:00:08,683
So, if we take on a breakout strategy and we

958
01:00:08,683 --> 01:00:10,750
do not see these expectations, so maybe

959
01:00:10,750 --> 01:00:13,703
we see a lot of two-way trade on a breakout.

960
01:00:13,705 --> 01:00:20,300
That is telling us straight away that this strategy is not playing out according to our expectations.

961
01:00:21,300 --> 01:00:26,905
Now, again, a lot of the time these expectations can actually get us out of good trades.

962
01:00:26,905 --> 01:00:32,240
But more often than not, they're going to protect us against the trade that's going to fail.

963
01:00:32,240 --> 01:00:35,820
And that is why we have an expectation analysis.

964
01:00:35,820 --> 01:00:42,280
So, those are some of the rules and preconditions you will develop for that trade strategy.

965
01:00:42,280 --> 01:00:44,262
Now, the reason we develop these rules

966
01:00:44,262 --> 01:00:46,527
and preconditions is because at the end of

967
01:00:46,527 --> 01:00:49,359
the day, when we go and analyze the trade strategy,

968
01:00:49,359 --> 01:00:55,873
when we go and look at how that trade actually played out, we need to have something we can benchmark the trade against.

969
01:00:55,875 --> 01:01:00,270
So, that we make sure that if it succeeds, we continuously improve.

970
01:01:00,270 --> 01:01:02,895
If it fails, we continuously improve.

971
01:01:02,895 --> 01:01:10,480
Ultimately, we're continuously improving the trade setup by being able to benchmark it against a set of parameters or rules.

972
01:01:13,560 --> 01:01:20,995
Now, once you have completed that, we then move on to the risk management and money management side of things.

973
01:01:20,995 --> 01:01:22,343
Ultimately, what you want to

974
01:01:22,343 --> 01:01:24,231
think about here is, how much of

975
01:01:24,231 --> 01:01:26,928
your day do you want to risk on this strategy?

976
01:01:26,930 --> 01:01:30,755
What sort of size do you want to put onto the strategy?

977
01:01:30,755 --> 01:01:35,570
How are you going to manage when you go on-side or off-side?

978
01:01:35,570 --> 01:01:37,385
Are you going to pyramid?

979
01:01:37,385 --> 01:01:39,220
Are you willing to average?

980
01:01:39,220 --> 01:01:42,420
Over what series of prices are you willing to get involved in?

981
01:01:42,420 --> 01:01:45,375
These are all sorts of considerations you

982
01:01:45,375 --> 01:01:48,752
need to take into account when determining your

983
01:01:48,752 --> 01:01:51,284
risk management and money management style.

984
01:01:51,285 --> 01:01:57,370
Now, again, different setups, different strategies will have completely different risk parameters.

985
01:01:57,370 --> 01:02:01,275
If you're doing a momentum trade setup, the risk is far tighter.

986
01:02:01,275 --> 01:02:02,885
If you're doing, say, a

987
01:02:02,885 --> 01:02:05,139
high-volume area setup, remember, we spoke about

988
01:02:05,139 --> 01:02:08,359
a lot of two-way trade and a more passive entry.

989
01:02:08,360 --> 01:02:12,440
Therefore, you might see a completely different risk profile.

990
01:02:12,440 --> 01:02:18,735
It's important to make sure you understand the risk profile for each individual strategy.

991
01:02:18,735 --> 01:02:23,535
Once you've done that, we then move on to the execution methodology.

992
01:02:23,535 --> 01:02:26,036
Now, this is one of the most

993
01:02:26,036 --> 01:02:28,537
crucial parts because every single price, every

994
01:02:28,537 --> 01:02:32,468
single increment in the market is effectively money gained or lost.

995
01:02:32,470 --> 01:02:36,700
So, the questions you need to be asking yourself is, how do you enter?

996
01:02:36,700 --> 01:02:39,220
Are you aggressive, like in the low-volume area?

997
01:02:39,220 --> 01:02:42,510
Are you more passive in the high-volume area? Okay?

998
01:02:42,510 --> 01:02:46,730
Are you entering with limit orders or are you entering with stop orders?

999
01:02:46,730 --> 01:02:53,520
Then, stop placement. Where in the strategy... In other words, the stop placement is very simple.

1000
01:02:53,520 --> 01:02:55,002
I get this question very often where

1001
01:02:55,002 --> 01:02:56,484
people ask, you know, where do you

1002
01:02:56,484 --> 01:02:58,178
stop out for a certain number of prices?

1003
01:02:58,180 --> 01:03:05,565
It's very simple and listen to me carefully. You stop out where the trade strategy is invalidated.

1004
01:03:05,565 --> 01:03:12,875
In other words, it's no longer validated by what you initially got into the trade for.

1005
01:03:12,875 --> 01:03:17,430
So, in simple terms, again, with the breakout trade setup that we spoke about,

1006
01:03:17,430 --> 01:03:19,797
if you're expecting an aggressive move up and

1007
01:03:19,797 --> 01:03:22,164
you're not expecting to go very far offside

1008
01:03:22,164 --> 01:03:23,939
because you are going with momentum,

1009
01:03:23,940 --> 01:03:26,045
the minute you do go offside or

1010
01:03:26,045 --> 01:03:28,150
the minute you do see that, you

1011
01:03:28,150 --> 01:03:32,360
know, two-way trade that's not expected, that is the point at which you exit.

1012
01:03:32,360 --> 01:03:34,645
Not the point of a predetermined stop.

1013
01:03:34,645 --> 01:03:36,835
Not this business of a trailing stop.

1014
01:03:36,835 --> 01:03:40,350
You exit where the trade strategy is no longer valid.

1015
01:03:40,350 --> 01:03:41,805
And that is what a stop is.

1016
01:03:41,805 --> 01:03:45,715
It's the point that stops you out of a trade that is no longer valid.

1017
01:03:45,715 --> 01:03:48,840
There is no hard-fast rule when it comes to stop placement.

1018
01:03:48,840 --> 01:03:53,400
The hard-fast rule is very simply, you get out when the trade is invalid.

1019
01:03:55,080 --> 01:03:59,780
Now, once you've done that, you then want to look at where you want to target.

1020
01:03:59,780 --> 01:04:02,985
In other words, where are you getting out of the trade and taking profit?

1021
01:04:02,985 --> 01:04:05,185
Again, there's no hard-fast rule.

1022
01:04:05,185 --> 01:04:10,350
There's no risk-reward of 2-to-1, 3-to-1, 4-to-1. That's what the textbooks teach you.

1023
01:04:10,350 --> 01:04:14,765
That's what these gurus teach you about taking 10-to-1 risk-reward.

1024
01:04:14,765 --> 01:04:17,495
Okay, I'm going to tell you something. That's all rubbish.

1025
01:04:17,495 --> 01:04:22,560
You take your profit where the market tells you to take your profit.

1026
01:04:22,560 --> 01:04:24,315
Again, what do I mean by that?

1027
01:04:24,315 --> 01:04:26,635
Let's talk about that breakout example again.

1028
01:04:26,635 --> 01:04:28,770
The market is going higher and higher and higher.

1029
01:04:28,770 --> 01:04:29,830
We can see the breakout.

1030
01:04:29,830 --> 01:04:32,215
We can see the initiative from buyers in the market.

1031
01:04:32,215 --> 01:04:37,735
The minute we see responsive selling enter the market, that's the point at which we exit.

1032
01:04:37,735 --> 01:04:40,555
That's the point at which we take our profits.

1033
01:04:40,555 --> 01:04:40,830
Why?

1034
01:04:40,830 --> 01:04:43,840
Because we can see sellers, other time-frame or other,

1035
01:04:43,840 --> 01:04:48,035
you know, participants stepping into the market taking the opposite side trade.

1036
01:04:48,035 --> 01:04:52,165
That is the point at which we need to get out of our breakout setup.

1037
01:04:52,165 --> 01:04:56,720
Not this business of targeting this area or getting out of that area.

1038
01:04:56,720 --> 01:04:59,935
We get out when the market tells us to get out.

1039
01:04:59,935 --> 01:05:08,530
Now, once you've done the execution methodology, we then move on to expectation analysis and emotional tracking.

1040
01:05:08,530 --> 01:05:09,870
So crucial.

1041
01:05:09,870 --> 01:05:13,740
Another important section which I'm not sure many of you have thought about before.

1042
01:05:13,740 --> 01:05:15,305
So, what is this?

1043
01:05:15,305 --> 01:05:17,580
Well, simply, what do you expect to see?

1044
01:05:17,580 --> 01:05:21,285
What's the kind of interaction, the speed and the price response?

1045
01:05:21,285 --> 01:05:23,810
We spoke a little bit about time in module 2.

1046
01:05:23,810 --> 01:05:28,990
We spoke about how crucial time is at measuring the success of a trade.

1047
01:05:28,990 --> 01:05:36,505
Remember, if you're trading a momentum type play, the time expected to see success is far slower than,

1048
01:05:36,505 --> 01:05:40,040
for instance, if you're trading, say, a bracketing high volume area trade.

1049
01:05:41,120 --> 01:05:45,340
This is crucial to understanding because when you have an expectation analysis,

1050
01:05:45,340 --> 01:05:47,500
when the market does what you don't

1051
01:05:47,500 --> 01:05:49,660
expect it to do, that's a very,

1052
01:05:49,660 --> 01:05:51,512
very early clue that something is

1053
01:05:51,512 --> 01:05:54,598
actually not quite like you thought it might have been.

1054
01:05:54,600 --> 01:05:56,365
That is the time you want to exit.

1055
01:05:56,365 --> 01:05:58,325
That is the time you want to reduce risk.

1056
01:05:58,325 --> 01:06:03,585
When there's uncertainty or a lower probability of success, that's where you exit.

1057
01:06:03,585 --> 01:06:06,023
You enter because there's a high probability

1058
01:06:06,023 --> 01:06:08,461
of an event occurring and you exit

1059
01:06:08,461 --> 01:06:10,899
because the probability of success is diminishing.

1060
01:06:12,160 --> 01:06:16,790
Now, what you then need to think about is, how do you feel with your risk and your stops?

1061
01:06:16,790 --> 01:06:21,085
Not many traders think about this in terms of their emotional tracking.

1062
01:06:21,085 --> 01:06:22,180
So what do I mean by this?

1063
01:06:23,300 --> 01:06:25,940
Certain traders are very good at sitting off-site.

1064
01:06:25,940 --> 01:06:27,925
Certain traders are very good at taking risk.

1065
01:06:27,925 --> 01:06:30,285
Certain traders don't mind going a long way off-site.

1066
01:06:30,285 --> 01:06:34,685
But then there are certain traders that hate taking risk. And that's okay, guys.

1067
01:06:34,685 --> 01:06:37,079
I'm not suggesting at any point that

1068
01:06:37,079 --> 01:06:39,473
you take any one of my eleven

1069
01:06:39,473 --> 01:06:43,235
strategies and employ them as though they are the Holy Grail.

1070
01:06:43,235 --> 01:06:45,130
You need to understand yourself.

1071
01:06:45,130 --> 01:06:48,895
You need to understand how you assess and how you take risk.

1072
01:06:48,895 --> 01:06:56,175
So every strategy you develop, understand the emotional aspects surrounding that strategy and the risk you're going to be taking.

1073
01:06:56,175 --> 01:06:59,555
If you struggle to sit in trades for a long time, that's fine.

